implementationPlan.description:
TESTS FIRST (red). Covers DoD 1-3. Load test-driven-development.

Start from the LANDED bytes of scripts/tests/local-runner-agents.test.mjs: R10d1's SH1-SH9 plus the rows R10d2 (AC) and R10d3 added. Keep every row. If R10d1 renamed a helper named below when it landed, make the same change to the landed equivalent.

1. Helpers.
- node:fs gains existsSync.
- TABLE = JSON.parse(readFileSync(TOOLS_FILE, 'utf8')): the checked-in typed table, role -> tool -> key -> fragment. PROJECTION (role -> tool -> TOOLS args) stays.
- listed(role) keeps the names and key order from TOOLS[role], but each property's schema becomes TABLE[role][name][key] instead of {}:
  {name, inputSchema: {type: 'object', properties: Object.fromEntries(args.map(key => [key, TABLE[role][name][key]])), additionalProperties: false}}
  SH1 and SH8 use it, so SH8 needs no other edit.
- launch() gains a `tools` option, default TOOLS_FILE, that replaces the TOOLS_FILE argv entry. Existing calls are unchanged.
- typed(schema) drops the description keyword at every schema level and keeps everything else in order:
  `const typed = ({ description, ...schema }) => ({ ...schema, ...(schema.items && { items: typed(schema.items) }), ...(schema.properties && { properties: Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(schema.properties).map(([key, value]) => [key, typed(value)])) }) });`
  Never use a JSON.stringify replacer on 'description': it would also delete qa_reject's issue PROPERTY named description.
- Optional: extract SH1's temp-file writes and its one load_tools spawnSync into helpers that TY3 reuses.

2. SH1 moves to the typed shape with the same behavior. It still pins the keys to TOOLS, serves exactly MOE_ROLE's tools, serves none to unknown roles, refuses every malformed table, and loads the table at the size bound.
- Replace the key-projection byte pin with two checks:
  (a) the file is exactly `${JSON.stringify(TABLE)}\n`, one compact line;
  (b) TABLE mapped to role -> tool -> Object.keys(fragments) deepEquals PROJECTION.
  The full pin to TOOLS plus the daemon is TY1's job.
- Translate the load_tools cases so each still isolates its own refusal. A tool value [] becomes {}, and a listed key becomes a typed fragment:
  - small = '{"worker":{"moe.x":{}}}';
  - {"moe.Get":{}} and {"get_context":{}};
  - duplicate key: {"moe.x":{"a":{"type":"string"},"a":{"type":"string"}}};
  - duplicate role: {"worker":{"moe.x":{}},"worker":{}};
  - bad key: {"moe.x":{"1a":{"type":"string"}}};
  - the [1] case becomes a non-object fragment, {"moe.x":{"a":1}};
  - the NaN case uses {}.
  '{', '[]', {"worker":[]}, {"moe.x":"view"}, the invalid UTF-8 case and both size cases are unchanged.
- The repo file's expected load_tools result becomes TABLE.

3. New rows under a '// CQ-R10d5 typed argument schemas' section at the end of the file.

TY1 (DoD 2): the drift test. It also runs on win32; timeout 60_000.
- DAEMON_TOOLS = new URL('../../packages/moe-daemon/dist/tools/index.js', import.meta.url).
- First: assert.ok(existsSync(DAEMON_TOOLS), 'build the daemon first: cd packages/moe-daemon && npm install && npm run build'). A missing build FAILS the row; it never skips.
- `const daemon = new Map((await import(DAEMON_TOOLS.href)).getTools(null).map(tool => [tool.name, tool.inputSchema]))`.
- For every role and tool of TOOLS:
  - daemon.get(tool)?.additionalProperties is false, with the tool name as the message;
  - every arg key is an own key of its properties, with the tool and key as the message.
- expected = role -> tool -> key -> typed(daemon property), in TOOLS order. Then: assert.equal(readFileSync(TOOLS_FILE, 'utf8'), `${JSON.stringify(expected)}\n`).
- Anchors on expected, so a typed() bug cannot pass with a file regenerated by the same bug:
  - complete_step.modifiedFiles = {type: 'array', items: {type: 'string'}};
  - complete_task.verification = {type: 'object', properties: {command: {type: 'string'}, exitCode: {type: 'number'}, outputTail: {type: 'string'}}, required: ['command', 'exitCode'], additionalProperties: false};
  - get_context.commentsLimit = {type: 'number'};
  - qa_reject.issues.items.properties.description = {type: 'string'}.
- A comment above TY1 says that tools.json is generated from TOOLS plus the built daemon's inputSchemas by exactly this projection, and is never hand-edited. It also says why the source is the built daemon and not src: heartbeat's ATTEMPT_PRESENCE_KINDS and qa_reject's VALID_ISSUE_TYPES enums exist only at runtime. CI builds the daemon before this step on all three OSes.

TY2 (DoD 1; skip NO_GUEST). For worker and qa, read the shim's real tools/list through exchange:
- per tool, [name, Object.keys(inputSchema.properties)] deepEquals TOOLS[role]'s [name, args], so the walk below is never empty;
- every inputSchema has type 'object' and additionalProperties false;
- a walk finds no property, array items or nested property without a string type. An array with no items counts as untyped. The flattened result must be []:
  `const untyped = (schema, path) => typeof schema?.type !== 'string' ? [path] : [...(schema.type === 'array' ? untyped(schema.items, `${path}[]`) : []), ...Object.entries(schema.properties ?? {}).flatMap(([key, value]) => untyped(value, `${path}.${key}`))];`

TY3 (DoD 3; skip NO_GUEST).
- Let S = {type: 'string'} and nest(n) = n === 1 ? S : {type: 'array', items: nest(n - 1)}. Write each case as JSON.stringify({worker: {'moe.x': M}}).
- load_tools returns {} for every M below:
  - ['a'], the R10d1 key-list shape;
  - {a: {}};
  - {a: {type: X}} for X 'date', 'null', ['string', 'null'] and 1;
  - {type: 'array'}, and {type: 'array', items: {}};
  - items on a string, and enum ['1'] on a number;
  - a string with description, default or $ref;
  - an object with no properties, with properties [], with {b: {}}, or with a property named '1b';
  - with properties {b: S}: required ['c'], ['b', 'b'], 'b' and [['b']];
  - additionalProperties {};
  - enum [], [1], ['a', 'a'], 'a' and [['a']];
  - nest(9).
- Positive control: GOOD = {s: {type: 'string', enum: ['a']}, n: {type: 'number'}, i: {type: 'integer'}, b: {type: 'boolean'}, d: nest(8), o: {type: 'object', properties: {c: S}, required: ['c'], additionalProperties: false}}. The table {worker: {'moe.x': GOOD, 'moe.y': {}}} loads unchanged (deepEqual).
- End to end: a shim launched with `tools` set to the {a: {}} file answers tools/list with {tools: []} and a tools/call of moe.get_context with TOOL_DENIED.

4. Run `node --test scripts/tests/local-runner-agents.test.mjs`; on WSL use the env -i form in step 3. Confirm each row is red for the right reason:
- SH1(b): the key lists give array indices;
- TY1: the bytes differ;
- TY2: the properties are {};
- TY3: the old shim loads ['a'] and refuses GOOD.
Every other row stays green.
